lessons.notes:
## Grid basics

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
  gap: 1rem;                 /* or row-gap / column-gap */
}
```

- **Grid lines** are numbered from 1; `-1` is the last line.
- **Tracks** are rows and columns; **cells** are their intersections; **areas** are rectangles of cells.

## Sizing tracks

| Value | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `200px` | fixed |
| `1fr` | a share of free space |
| `auto` | size to content |
| `minmax(150px, 1fr)` | between a min and a max |
| `repeat(3, 1fr)` | repeat a pattern |
| `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr))` | responsive columns |

`auto-fill` keeps empty columns; `auto-fit` collapses them so items stretch.

## Placing items

```css
.hero   { grid-column: 1 / -1; }         /* full width */
.tall   { grid-row: span 2; }
.box    { grid-column: 2 / 4; grid-row: 1; }
```

## Named template areas

```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  min-height: 100vh;
  grid-template:
    "header header" auto
    "nav    main"   1fr
    "footer footer" auto / 220px 1fr;
}
header { grid-area: header; }
nav    { grid-area: nav; }
main   { grid-area: main; }
footer { grid-area: footer; }
```

A `.` in the map leaves a cell empty.

## Alignment

`justify-items` / `align-items` align content inside cells; `place-items: center` does both. `justify-content` / `align-content` align the whole grid inside the container.

## Grid or flexbox?

- **Grid**: page layouts, galleries, anything with rows **and** columns.
- **Flexbox**: a single row or column of items, like a toolbar.
- They combine well: a grid page whose header uses flexbox.

`subgrid` (supported in all modern browsers) lets nested grids align to the parent's tracks.

